    760	| §D.D4 ATTACK_VECTORS — Szegedy quantum walks on number-theoretic graphs (cross-domain: Szegedy 2004 discriminant matrix theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether Szegedy walks on a number-theoretic graph yield polylog primality extraction. Tested **three families** with the discriminant matrix `D(x,y) = sqrt(P(x,y)·P(y,x))` and the eigenvalue-lifting theorem `cos(θ_k) → e^{±2iθ_k}` (mixing time `O(1/sqrt(δ))`). **(A) Cayley `Cay((Z/NZ)*, {2,3,5,inv})`** for primes N∈{31,…,1009} (14 sizes): empirical `t_classical(N) ~ N^{0.789}`, `t_szegedy(N) ~ N^{0.414}` — quadratic Szegedy speedup empirically realised but neither is polylog. Direct **quantum extension of E7.12 (S79)**: classical Cayley spectrum probes ω(n) not primality, AND Szegedy quantisation inherits the same poly-mixing barrier. **(B) Coprime `C_x = ([1..x], gcd=1)`** for x∈{30,…,1000}: spectral gap is `Ω(1)` (asymptotically `δ → 0.4166...`); stationary prime mass / uniform prime mass `→ ζ(2) = π²/6 ≈ 1.6449` (Mertens; verified at x=1000 with deviation -0.022). Both classical and Szegedy mix in `O(1)`, but bias is constant — `O(log x)` samples to hit a prime + AKS verification = no polylog extractor of π(x). **(C) Divisor `D_x = ([1..x], m|n)`**: gap `~Ω(1)` (slowly decaying 0.21 → 0.166 across x=30..1000). High-prime-mass eigenvectors localise on **prime-centered divisor clusters** `{p, 2p, 3p, ..., 1}` for *individual* primes near x/3-x/2 (e.g., k=14 eigenvector at x=250 is concentrated 100% on {43, 47} ∪ multiples ∪ {1}). Each eigenvector identifies 1-2 specific primes — same structural mechanism as E7.12 (degree class probing), not global primality. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: empirical confirmation `t_Q ≈ sqrt(t_C)` for the Szegedy-Cayley case validates the discriminant theorem at the project's scale; the π²/6 stationary identity is a clean Mertens-law derivation specific to the coprime-graph chain. **Falsification check**: no graph yields mixing time `O(polylog(x))` AND a primality-localised eigenvector with poly-log gap; both Cayley (poly mixing) and coprime/divisor (Ω(1) mixing but no global primality eigenvector) miss the joint condition. Closes ATTACK_VECTORS §D.D4. **Suggested EDGE** (E7.13 candidate): "Szegedy-walk quantisation of any reversible chain on `[1..x]` with adjacency defined by simple arithmetic relation does not yield polylog primality extraction — either spectral gap shrinks polynomially (Cayley class) or eigenvectors capture primality only via degree-class clustering (coprime / divisor class). The two conditions are exhaustive on the families tested." Cites E5.3, E5.8, E7.10, E7.12. Cross-domain reference: Szegedy 2004 (arxiv quant-ph/0401053). See experiments/quantum/szegedy_walk_prime_graphs/. | 80 |
    761	| C6 (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES) — three-pillars × HKM time-space tradeoff classification | REFINEMENT | I | Composition of E7.7 (three-pillars informational closure: prime positions / zeta zeros / floor values) and E6.7 (HKM at (T = N^{8/15}, S = N^{1/3})). Built (alpha, beta) catalog of 14 algorithms (4 prime / 4 zeta / 6 floor) and computed per-pillar Pareto frontiers, cross-pillar dominance, and saturation against E7.6 lower bound (T*S ≥ Ω(N^{5/6}/log N)). **Findings**: (i) HKM is on the floor-pillar Pareto frontier and DOMINATES every other floor-pillar entry elementwise (Lucy DP, Lucy+Fenwick, Meissel-Lehmer classical, Gourdon, primecount); (ii) HKM has NO cross-pillar dominators — its (8/15, 1/3) point is uniquely accessible on the floor pillar (no zero-pillar or prime-pillar entry achieves both T ≤ N^{8/15} AND S ≤ N^{1/3}); (iii) **pillar dominance regions are non-overlapping**: time-only minimum is shared by prime/zeta pillars at α=1/2, space-only minimum is unique to floor at β=1/3, T*S minimum is unique to floor at 13/15 ≈ 0.867; (iv) HKM saturates E7.6 to within N^{0.034} (gap 13/15 - 5/6 = 1/30); (v) Aggarwal binary search (E6.6) is a meta-algorithm whose effective placement migrates with the chosen pi(x) sub-routine. All four pre-stated falsification criteria (F1: HKM dominated; F2: HKM cross-pillar dominated; F3: identical pillar frontiers; F4: floor-pillar T*S < 5/6) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) explicit pillar-by-pillar Pareto map of the project's catalog; (b) HKM uniqueness lemma (HKM's (T, S) point is achievable on the floor pillar ONLY); (c) saturation verdict (floor pillar asymptotically tight). No polylog opening — refinement of E7.7 and E6.7, not a new edge. Cites E5.1, E6.5, E6.6, E6.7, E6.8, E6.9, E7.6, E7.7, E7.9. See experiments/constructions/pillar_tradeoff_diagram/. | 81 |
    762	| §A.A1 ATTACK_VECTORS — SAT/ILP-based search for small TC^0 (depth-2 sign-threshold W=1) circuits computing PRIMES at N≤8 (cross-domain: SAT-based circuit synthesis, Niemetz et al.) | FAIL | I | Wild-swing on §A1: enumerate small TC^0 circuits matching PRIMES at N=8 via SAT/ILP. Built three solvers (Z3 If-then-else, direct ILP via PuLP+CBC, pre-enumerated column-selection ILP); the pre-enumeration approach is 5-10x faster. **Exact PTF degrees** (LP feasibility on monomial basis, real coefficients): PRIMES at N=4..8 → 2, 3, 3, 3, **4**; pi(x) mod 2 → 3, 3, 3, 4, 4; matched random median over 5 seeds → 2, 3, 3, 3, 4. PRIMES PTF degree statistically indistinguishable from random — confirms E1.10/E3.13 at the Boolean-function level. **Exact depth-2 sign-threshold (W=1 both layers) sizes**: N=4 → M=3, N=6 → M=6 (full kmax candidate set, 1458 W=1 thresholds). N=7 inconclusive (M=5, 6 unsat; M=7, 8 timed out at 180s). N=8: M ≥ 17 with kmax≤5 candidate set (M=8, 10, 12, 14 unsat with kmax=4; M=16 unsat with kmax=5; M=20 timed out). **Statistically significant PRIMES-vs-random gap at N=6**: PRIMES needs M=6 bottom gates; ALL 10 random matched controls (weight 18/64) need M ∈ {7, 8} (4 seeds → M=7, 6 seeds → M=8). Binomial null p < 0.001. **First instance in the project of a circuit-complexity measure where PRIMES empirically deviates from a matched-density random function** (35+ prior pseudorandomness measures show no deviation). **Mechanism**: PRIMES has an exceptionally strong single-bit predictor (bit_0 = "is x odd") with 70.3% accuracy at N=8, vs random's best 1-bit at 57.0% (max over 30 seeds). The depth-2 circuit gets the 1-bit predictor "for free" as the first bottom gate; subsequent gates correct fewer remaining errors → smaller M. The PRIMES-vs-random depth-2 gap REDUCES TO the elementary "most primes are odd" fact (1-bit advantage of 0.13 absolute at N=8). At N=4 the gap is absent (random can match) because the small domain admits accidental 1-bit predictors; at N≥6 the gap is robust because no random function of similar weight has such a strong 1-bit bias. **Bottom-layer gates of found solutions are empirical fits**, not number-theoretic primality witnesses (no Miller-Rabin / BPSW / AKS interpretation). **Closure mode I** (information loss): the depth-2 W=1 sign-threshold complexity at N=8 is super-linear in N (M_PRIMES ≥ 17 vs N=8), strongly suggesting exponential or super-polynomial scaling not polylog. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: SAT/ILP search across 35K candidate functions enabled exact bounds infeasible by hand. Refines CLOSED_PATHS line "Nonlinear sieve: bitwise/TC^0" (S14) with quantitative complexity numbers. ATTACK_VECTORS §A1 is **NOT closed by this session** (depth-5 size-2000 enumeration intractable with current tools); the W=1 sign-threshold sub-family is closed at N=8 with the ≥17-gate lower bound. Cites E1.10, E3.13, E5.3, E7.10, S20, S28, novel/pseudorandomness_of_pi.md (now requires footnote: 36th measure DEVIATES with elementary mechanism). See experiments/circuit_complexity/sat_tc0_primes_n8/. | 84 |
    763	| S148 commit-thread step 1 — S82 follow-on |L(1, chi)|^2 eigenvalue conjecture REFUTED, replaced by Gallagher-variance scaling sigma^2 ~ N/(p log^2 N) | REFUTATION+DISCOVERY | C | Commit-thread session 1/5 on the S82 invariant-subspace theorem. **S82 conjectured**: spike eigenvectors of `M^T M` are Dirichlet character vectors `chi_lift(n) = chi(n mod q)` with eigenvalue `sigma^2 ~ |L(1, chi)|^2`. **Two-prong empirical test against S82's saved JSON dumps at d ∈ {14, 18, 20}, W=2.** **PR1 (S82's hypothesis)**: ratio `(sum sigma^2 in q-block) / (sum |L(1, chi)|^2 over phi(p)-1 non-principal chars mod p)` constant across `p ∈ {3, 5, 7, 11}`. Computed |L(1, chi)|^2 via closed-form `L(1, chi) = -(1/p) sum_a chi(a) psi(a/p)` (digamma; Davenport MNT Ch.6) for all chars mod p ∈ {3, 5, 7, 11, 13}. **PR1 FAILS at d=20**: ratios = (18246, 2093, 813, 539) across p=(3,5,7,11), CV = 1.37 (>>0.2 threshold). **PR2 (alternative)**: rescaled `K(d, p) := sum sigma^2 · p · log^2(N) / N` constant across saturated (d, p). **PR2 PASSES**: K = (4.48, 3.83, 3.79, 4.08, 3.67, 3.38, 3.78) across (d,p) ∈ {(14,3), (18,3), (18,5), (18,7), (20,3), (20,5), (20,7)}, mean 3.86, std 0.32, CV 0.082. **Within-block per-character resolution test**: at d=20, mod-7 block sigma^2 spread max/min = 1.10× while corresponding |L(1, chi)|^2 spread 4.70× — within-block sigma values are nearly equal across the phi(p) spikes, NOT correlated with individual character L-values. **Net new content**: (a) **S82's eigenvalue conjecture sigma^2 ~ |L(1,chi)|^2 is decisively refuted** at the per-block AND per-character level; the residue-class character IDENTIFICATION stands, but the eigenvalue formula does not. (b) **Empirical replacement formula**: `sum_block sigma^2 ≈ 3.86 · N / (p log^2 N) ≈ 3.86 · pi(N)/(p log N) ≈ 3.86 · pi(N; p, *)` — a Gallagher-Montgomery-Vaughan PNT-in-AP variance scaling, NOT an L-function-value formula. (c) **Sharper C-circular interpretation**: the spike eigenvalues are determined by the second-moment of pi(N; q, a) over residue classes (Gallagher 1970, Montgomery-Vaughan MNT I Ch.17), so the C2 spectral barrier reduces to the Gallagher 2nd-moment — a quantity itself controlled by Bombieri-Vinogradov/PNT-in-AP, with no polylog method. (d) Closes the "look-up L(1, chi) to compute pi(N) via spectral compression" route hoped for by S82. **Closure mode C** (circular): to compute `sum_block sigma^2` one would need pi(N; p, *) for small p, which is itself the PNT-in-AP problem. Cites E2.1, E1.5, S74, S82. Cross-domain refs: Gallagher 1970 *Mathematika 23*; Montgomery-Vaughan 2007 *MNT I* Ch.17; Davenport 2000 *MNT* Ch.6 (closed-form L(1, chi) via digamma). See experiments/constructions/spike_eigenvalue_l_squared/. | 148 |
    764	| Spike eigenvectors of chi_P MPS unfolding identified as Dirichlet character vectors at small odd primes — C2 sub-arc S82 sharpening of S74 | REFINEMENT | C | Sub-arc closure of S74 (free_cumulants_chi_p): the polynomial-in-N "spike band" of `M^(j) M^(j)^T` eigenvalues found in S74 decomposes structurally as **direct sum of residue-class character subspaces at small odd primes coprime to W**. For each top singular pair `(σ_k, u_k, v_k)`, reconstructed `f_k(n) = σ_k · u_k(i) · v_k(j')` at `n = i·W^{d-j} + j' + 1` and computed centered residue-mod-q energy fraction `E_q(k) = ‖P_q(f_k - mean)‖² / ‖f_k - mean‖²` for q ∈ {2..200}; defined `min_q_conductor(k)` = smallest q achieving 95% of `max_q E_q(k)`. **Empirical pattern at (W=2, d=20, k_*=26)**: spikes 1–2 → conductor 6 (= 2·3, count = φ(3) = 2 ✓); spikes 3–6 → conductor 10 (= 2·5, count = φ(5) = 4 ✓); spikes 7–12 → conductor 14 (= 2·7, count = φ(7) = 6 ✓); spikes 13–20 → conductor 30 (= 2·3·5 cross, count = φ(15) = 8 ✓); spikes 21–25 → conductor ~88 (= 8·11, partial mod-11). Saturation across d ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20}: each prime sector first appears as "transitional" partial at small N then locks in at exactly φ(p) once `N/log N ≫ p · φ(p)`. **PR1 partial-PASS** (refined): max-q energy thresholded at >100× baseline rather than >0.5 absolute. **PR2 PASS**: matched-active-Bernoulli baseline at d=20 has `max_q E_q ≤ 5×10⁻⁴` for all spike candidates; chi_P spikes ≥ 10⁻², ratio ≥ 20×. **PR3 PASS**: visited-q dimension sums to k_* within factor 1.5 (25 observed vs 26 predicted). **Cross-W check (W=6, d=6, k_*=7)**: mod-3 sector ABSENT (W incorporates 3); spikes start at conductor 30 (mod-5, count 4 ✓) then conductor 42 (mod-7, count 6-7 ≈ φ(7)). **Net new content**: (a) precise structural identification of the S74 spike eigenvectors as residue-class character vectors at small primes p ≤ P*(N) with P*(N) ≈ N^{0.21}; (b) PNT-consistent prediction `k_* ≈ N^{0.42} / log N` with explicit per-prime φ(p) accounting; (c) **C-circular collapse of C2's algorithmic implication**: the polynomial-in-N rank barrier from S74 IS the small-modulus residue-class bias content `pi(N; q, a)`, exactly the regime E1.5 saturation governs — the spectral barrier and the information-theoretic barrier are the *same object* viewed from opposite sides. (d) E2.1 annotated S82 with the structural identification. **No polylog opening**: T3 / failure mode C — to compute the spike subspace requires already knowing `pi(N; q, a)` at small q. Cites E2.1 (annotated S74, S77, S82), E1.5, S74 (free_cumulants_chi_p). Cross-domain references: Iwaniec-Kowalski 2004 Ch. 3 (Dirichlet characters), Rubinstein-Sarnak 1994 (Chebyshev's bias = the residue-class bias the spikes quantify). See experiments/constructions/spike_eigenvectors_chi_p/. | 82 |
    765	| §D6 ATTACK_VECTORS — Gowers U^k uniformity norms of the bare prime indicator chi_P (cross-domain: additive combinatorics, Green-Tao-Ziegler U^{s+1} inverse theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether chi_P deviates from random pseudorandomly under any Gowers norm at k <= 3 — the only family of "k-degree polynomial structure" tests untouched by the project's 35+ pseudorandomness measures. Computed normalized Gowers norms `Q^k(f) := ‖f‖_{U^k}^{2^k} / E[f]^{2^k}` on Z/NZ for chi_P, matched-density Bernoulli, Liouville indicator, and W-tricked chi_{W,1} (W ∈ {6, 30, 210}). U^2 via FFT identity `‖f‖_{U^2}^4 = (1/N^4) Σ_k |fhat(k)|^4` (O(N log N)) at N ∈ {2^10, 2^12, 2^14, 2^16, 2^18}; U^3 via recursion `‖f‖_{U^3}^8 = (1/N) Σ_h ‖Δ_h f‖_{U^2}^4` with `Δ_h f(x) = f(x)f(x+h)` (O(N^2 log N)) at N ∈ {2^10, 2^11, 2^12, 2^13}. **Hardy-Littlewood prediction**: `Q^k(chi_P) → S_k = Π_p α_p(k) / (1 - 1/p)^{2^k}` (singular series for {0,1}^k-cube prime k-tuple), where `α_p(k) = #{(x, h) ∈ (Z/p)^{k+1} : x + ε·h ≢ 0 mod p ∀ε ∈ {0,1}^k} / p^{k+1}`. Numerical truncated values: **S_2 = 2.300938** (P_max=100, 1e-5 truncation error), **S_3 = 54.116088** (P_max=47, 0.2% error). **Empirical Q^2(chi_P) trend**: 2.103 (N=2^10) → 2.132 (N=2^12) → 2.146 (N=2^14) → 2.149 (N=2^16) → 2.153 (N=2^18); monotonic increase toward S_2; gap S_2 - Q^2(chi_P) decays as O(1/log N) (from 0.198 at N=2^10 to 0.148 at N=2^18). **Q^2(Bernoulli) ~ 1 + O(1/N)** (1.049 → 1.008 across same range). **Q^3(chi_P) ≈ 35.5** stable across N ∈ [2^10, 2^13], well above Bernoulli's Q^3 → 1 but below S_3 = 54.12 due to slow finite-N convergence at U^3 (compounded log-factors). **Liouville**: Q^2(L) = 1.000 to 4 digits at N=2^10..2^12 — Gowers-uniform at U^2, consistent with Green-Tao Mobius/nilsequence orthogonality (the prime-parity indicator is structurally OPPOSITE to chi_P at U^k). **W-trick**: predicted `S^(W)_k = Π_{p∤W} α_p(k)/(1-1/p)^{2^k}`. At N=2^12, **W=210**: empirical Q^2(chi_{W,1}) = 1.0029 vs HL prediction 1.0023 (match within 0.1%); empirical Q^3 = 1.066 vs HL 1.029 (match within 4%, dominated by finite-N noise on Q^3). **W=30**: Q^2 = 1.005 vs 1.007, exact match. The W-trick empirically restores Gowers-uniformity to chi_P, exactly as the Green-Tao W-trick predicts for Lambda. **First instance in the project's pseudorandomness battery (after S84 circuit-depth gap at N=6) where chi_P deviates from random by a CLOSED-FORM, predicted, constant multiplicative factor**, with the deviation EXACTLY matching the Hardy-Littlewood box singular series. **Closure mode E**: U^k structure of chi_P provides only HL-level information, already conjecturally known; no additional bit of structure detected; algorithmic extraction of Q^k itself costs Θ(N^2 log N), not polylog. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: Gowers norms (Gowers 2001) detect higher-order correlation structure invisible to the project's local statistics; the FFT identity for U^2 and recursion for U^3 are standard machinery from additive combinatorics. **Cited new EDGE: E2.13** (Q^k(chi_P) = S_k {0,1}^k-cube). Cites E1.10, E3.13, E7.1, E2.9, S84. Cross-domain refs: Gowers (2001); Green-Tao (arXiv:math/0606088); Green-Tao (arXiv:0807.1736); Green-Tao-Ziegler (arXiv:1009.3998); Hardy-Littlewood (1923). See experiments/information_theory/gowers_uk_chi_p_results.md. | 85 |
